[
    {
        "content": "<p>In <a href=\"https://github.com/RemyDegenne/brownian-motion/pull/399\">PR399</a> (files Komlos.lean, ConvexWeights.lean) I've been working on formalizing the construction of Komlos weights <a href=\"https://remydegenne.github.io/brownian-motion/blueprint/chap-doob_meyer.html#lem:komlos_convex_weights\">(blueprint lemma)</a>. The proof is largely finished on a conceptual level, with some small changes/sorries remaining that I think won't affect the overall structure of the argument. I also still need to clean up a few things. </p>\n<p>Thinking ahead, I'm wondering what the most modular way to formalize convex weights will be. Currently, I represent them through finitely supported maps <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi><msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w : \\mathbb{N} \\to_0 \\R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02691em;\">w</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">N</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span>  and then manually state that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02691em;\">w</span></span></span></span> has to be nonnegative and sum up to 1. </p>\n<p>The reason for using <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span> over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\R\\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8249em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> is that the lemma <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/Convex/Combination.html#Finset.mem_convexHull'\">Finset.mem_convexHull'</a> which I was planning to use to obtain explicit convex weights requires typeclasses that don't exist for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\R\\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8249em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>. I suppose if one wanted to use <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\R\\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8249em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> one could write a bridging lemma though. </p>\n<p>I'm wondering if a bundled definition would be desirable, i.e. one would write <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi><msub><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w : \\N \\to_{convex} \\R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02691em;\">w</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">N</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">co</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">v</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">e</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">x</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span> or similar and this would automatically ensure that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02691em;\">w</span></span></span></span> sums up to 1 and is nonnegative. This would likely make my current formalisation more elegant, since many of the side conditions would simply become part of the type signature. But I'm not sure if a bundled definition would help in other projects, too. If not it might be a bit overkill. </p>\n<p>Right now, the lemma Finset.mem_convexHull I mention above does not get used in any proofs in Mathlib (is there a way to search if it gets used in downstream projects?). It would be good to compare what other people do with convex weights before making the design decision whether to bundle the definition or not. What do you think?</p>",
        "id": 588675681,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1776790379
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Does it make sense to state it with PMFs?</p>",
        "id": 590390675,
        "sender_full_name": "Kexing Ying",
        "timestamp": 1776932549
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The weights I consider are always finite (they might be indexed by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb N</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">N</span></span></span></span>, but are only nonzero for finitely many values). As far as I can see, Mathlib's <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/72acbe66661886ce1ff53b41ae74c8a2f0b52ade/Mathlib/Probability/ProbabilityMassFunction/Basic.lean#L44-L47\">PMF</a> is designed for infinite sums so I don't think this would help for my proofs. Unless there is also a finite version of PMF, which I've not been able to find.</p>",
        "id": 590409275,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1776937284
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=StdSimplex#doc\">docs#StdSimplex</a></p>",
        "id": 590509721,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1776959714
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks that's very helpful, I'll use StdSimplex then!</p>",
        "id": 590511027,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1776960029
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I wish I had found that def when preparing the blueprint last month! Thanks a lot Bhavik!</p>",
        "id": 590636806,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1777014297
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I just made my first Mathlib PR (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38758\">#38758</a>) adding StdSimplex.bind and necessary API lemmas, any feedback is welcome! </p>\n<p>I'm wondering if it would make sense to also introduce notation for StdSimplex. Writing <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msub><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I \\rightarrow_1 R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msub><mo>→</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I \\rightarrow_s R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">s</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span> for convex weights from an index set <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span> might be nicer and would mirror the Finsupp notation.</p>",
        "id": 592011900,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1777543725
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you add it <a href=\"#narrow/channel/509433-Brownian-motion/topic/Mathlib.20PRs/with/586353599\">here</a> please?</p>",
        "id": 592040450,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1777552079
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I just opened another <a href=\"https://github.com/RemyDegenne/brownian-motion/pull/435/changes\">PR</a> related to the Komlos lemma that involves some uniform convergence argument. </p>\n<p>The goal is to show <code>komlos_convex_weights_diagonal</code> for which I have an (effectively) complete proof, but it relies on <code>komlos_uniform_convergence_epsilon</code> which doesn't use <code>TendstoUniformly</code> but just spells out what uniform convergence means as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall \\epsilon &gt; 0, ...</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ϵ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">...</span></span></span></span>. The statement should be equivalent to <code>komlos_uniform_convergence</code> which does use <code>TendstoUniformly</code>.</p>\n<p>What is the best practice in a situation like this, would a filter-based proof with <code>TendstoUniformly</code> be preferred? In general, I feel like I understand the equivalence between filters and epsilon-statements, but find it difficult to express proofs in the filter language when the situation is more involved (note that my example here involves uniform convergence over a subtype <code>{ k : ℕ // i ≤ k }</code>). Are there any ressources on this topic beyond what is written in Mathematics in Lean?</p>",
        "id": 593243659,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1778070505
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Instead of <code>TendstoUniformly</code> on a subtype, could you use <code>TendstoUniformlyOn</code>? Avoiding the use of subtypes is generally a good practice. Then the equivalence with your other statement should follow from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Pseudo/Basic.html#Metric.tendstoUniformlyOn_iff\">Metric.tendstoUniformlyOn_iff</a> and a lemma about eventually at top in Nat. it's fine to use TendstoUniformlyOn in the statement and then start the proof by translating to the epsilon statement.</p>",
        "id": 593255158,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1778073343
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for the suggestion! I don't think <code>TendstoUniformlyOn</code> is exactly what I need, but your suggestion made me realise that I can probably get rid of the subtype in a different way. Most of the work will be related to formalising statements on convex combinations though, so I think it makes sense pausing this work until <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37592\">#37592</a> gets merged.</p>",
        "id": 593527339,
        "sender_full_name": "Jonas Bayer",
        "timestamp": 1778161127
    }
]